## Why it fails
| Line | What happens |
|---|---|
| `let first = &v[0];` | Creates an **immutable borrow** of `v` — `first` holds a reference into the vector's heap storage. |
| `v.push(4);` | Requires a **mutable borrow** of `v`. A push may reallocate the heap buffer, which would **invalidate every existing reference** into the old allocation. |
| `println!("{}", first);` | Uses the now-stale reference. |The borrow checker sees that `first`'s lifetime extends across `push()`. Since `first` points into `v`'s internal storage, allowing a mutable operation that could reallocate would create a **dangling pointer**. Rust rejects this at compile time — no runtime cost.

## Fix 1 — Copy the value (simplest)
```rustfn main() {
    let mut v = vec![1, 2, 3];
    let first = v[0];        // i32 implements Copy — copies the value, no reference
    v.push(4);
    println!("{}", first);   // perfectly fine
}
```
## Fix 2 — Restructure to avoid overlapping borrows
```rustfn main() {
    let mut v = vec![1, 2, 3];
    {
        let first = &v[0];
        println!("{}", first);   // print while borrow is still alive
    }                              // borrow ends here
    v.push(4);                     // now safe — no active borrows
}
```
Both compile. Fix 1 is idiomatic for `Copy` types; Fix 2 preserves the reference pattern for non-`Copy` types (e.g. `String`).
